#: ../../../usage.rst:6
msgid "Why should I use scikit-build ?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:8
msgid ""
"Scikit-build is a replacement for `setuptools.Extension <https://setuptools."
"pypa.io/en/latest/userguide/ext_modules.html>`_ that builds extension "
"modules with CMake, providing:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:12
msgid ""
"better support for :doc:`additional compilers and build systems </"
"generators>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:13
msgid "first-class :ref:`cross-compilation <cross_compilation>` support"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:14
msgid "location of dependencies and their associated build requirements"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:16
msgid ""
"Scikit-build is a lightweight wrapper around the setuptools plugin of "
"`scikit-build-core <https://scikit-build-core.readthedocs.io>`_. Use it when "
"you have an existing ``setup.py``-based project or need setuptools features; "
"for new projects, use scikit-build-core directly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:33
msgid "Migrating from scikit-build 0.x"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:35
msgid ""
"Starting with scikit-build 1.0, the classic scikit-build backend was "
"replaced by the setuptools plugin provided by `scikit-build-core <https://"
"scikit-build-core.readthedocs.io>`_. ``skbuild.setup()`` is now a thin "
"wrapper around ``scikit_build_core.setuptools.wrapper.setup()``. Most "
"projects keep working unchanged:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:41
msgid ""
"``from skbuild import setup`` with the ``cmake_args``, ``cmake_source_dir``, "
"``cmake_install_dir``, ``cmake_install_target`` and "
"``cmake_process_manifest_hook`` keyword arguments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:44
msgid ""
"The CMake modules shipped with scikit-build, like "
"``find_package(PythonExtensions)``, ``find_package(Cython)``, "
"``find_package(NumPy)`` and ``find_package(F2PY)``. They are now injected "
"into ``CMAKE_MODULE_PATH`` using scikit-build-core's ``cmake.module`` entry-"
"point group."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:49
msgid ""
"The ``SKBUILD`` CMake variable is still set, now to ``2`` instead of "
"``TRUE`` (still truthy)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:51
msgid ""
"The standard setuptools commands: ``build``, ``bdist_wheel``, ``sdist``, "
"``install`` and ``build_ext --inplace``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:53
msgid ""
"The classic ``build-system`` table: ``requires = [\"setuptools\", \"scikit-"
"build\", \"cmake\", \"ninja\"]`` with ``build-backend = \"setuptools."
"build_meta\"``. The recommended backend is now ``scikit_build_core."
"setuptools.build_meta``, which adds ``cmake`` and ``ninja`` only when needed "
"and supports config-settings; see :ref:`basic_usage_example`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:60
msgid "The following changes are breaking:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:62
msgid ""
"All scikit-build-specific command line options were removed: ``--build-"
"type``, ``-G``/``--generator``, ``-j N``, ``--cmake-executable``, ``--skip-"
"generator-test``, ``--hide-listing``, ``--force-cmake``, ``--skip-cmake``, "
"``--install-target``, as well as the ``setup.py <setuptools args> -- <cmake "
"args> -- <build tool args>`` triple-section syntax. Use the ``CMAKE_ARGS`` "
"and ``CMAKE_GENERATOR`` environment variables, the ``cmake_args`` keyword of "
"``setup()``, or scikit-build-core's ``[tool.scikit-build]`` settings in "
"``pyproject.toml`` (or the equivalent ``SKBUILD_*`` environment variables or "
"config-settings) instead. See the `scikit-build-core configuration "
"documentation <https://scikit-build-core.readthedocs.io/en/latest/"
"configuration/index.html>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:74
msgid ""
"``cmake_with_sdist=True`` now raises an error. ``cmake_languages`` and "
"``cmake_minimum_required_version`` are accepted but ignored with a warning; "
"the minimum CMake version is configured with the ``cmake.version`` setting "
"in the ``[tool.scikit-build]`` table of ``pyproject.toml``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:79
msgid ""
"The internal Python API is gone; only ``skbuild.setup`` and ``skbuild."
"exceptions.SKBuildError`` remain public. ``skbuild.command.*`` and ``skbuild."
"platform_specifics`` were removed. ``skbuild.cmaker``, ``skbuild."
"constants``, ``skbuild.utils`` and ``skbuild.setuptools_wrap`` remain "
"importable as deprecated shims that warn on import: ``skbuild.cmaker`` keeps "
"only ``get_cmake_version()``, ``skbuild.constants`` keeps only "
"``CMAKE_INSTALL_DIR()`` and ``skbuild_plat_name()``, and the last two expose "
"nothing. ``skbuild.exceptions.SKBuildError`` is now an alias of setuptools' "
"``SetupError``, so it is no longer a ``RuntimeError``; its "
"``SKBuildInvalidFileInstallationError`` and "
"``SKBuildGeneratorNotFoundError`` subclasses were removed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:92
msgid ""
"The ``_skbuild/<platform>-<pyversion>/`` build directory is gone; the "
"standard setuptools ``build/`` directories are used instead (CMake builds in "
"an ``_skbuild`` directory under ``build/temp.*``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:96
msgid ""
"sdists no longer automatically generate their file manifest from git; "
"provide a ``MANIFEST.in`` (or use ``setuptools-scm``) like any other "
"setuptools project."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:100
msgid ""
"Editable installs (``pip install -e .``) require setting ``editable.mode = "
"\"inplace\"`` in the ``[tool.scikit-build]`` table of ``pyproject.toml``. A "
"plain ``setup.py build_ext --inplace`` still works without configuration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:105
msgid ""
"Generators are no longer discovered by probing for Visual Studio and running "
"a language test; CMake's own default generator selection applies. Set the "
"``CMAKE_GENERATOR`` environment variable to override it. See :doc:`/"
"generators`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:110
msgid ""
"scikit-build now depends on ``scikit-build-core[setuptools]`` at run time; "
"the ``distro``, ``wheel`` and ``tomli`` dependencies were dropped."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:115
msgid "Basic Usage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:120
msgid "Example of setup.py, CMakeLists.txt and pyproject.toml"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:121
msgid ""
"The full example code is `Here <https://github.com/scikit-build/scikit-build-"
"sample-projects/tree/master/projects/hello-cpp>`_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:123
msgid ""
"Make a folder named ``my_project`` as your project root folder, and place "
"the following in your project's ``setup.py`` file::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:126
msgid ""
"from skbuild import setup  # This line replaces 'from setuptools import "
"setup'\n"
"setup(\n"
"    name=\"hello-cpp\",\n"
"    version=\"1.2.3\",\n"
"    description=\"a minimal example package (cpp version)\",\n"
"    author='The scikit-build team',\n"
"    license=\"MIT\",\n"
"    packages=['hello'],\n"
"    python_requires=\">=3.10\",\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:137
msgid "Your project now uses scikit-build instead of setuptools."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:139
msgid ""
"Next, add a ``CMakeLists.txt`` to describe how to build your extension. In "
"the following example, a C++ extension named ``_hello`` is built::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:142
msgid ""
"cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.18...3.22)\n"
"\n"
"project(hello)\n"
"\n"
"find_package(PythonExtensions REQUIRED)\n"
"\n"
"add_library(_hello MODULE hello/_hello.cxx)\n"
"python_extension_module(_hello)\n"
"install(TARGETS _hello LIBRARY DESTINATION hello)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:152
msgid "Then, add a ``pyproject.toml`` declaring the build backend::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:154
msgid ""
"[build-system]\n"
"requires = [\"scikit-build>=1\"]\n"
"build-backend = \"scikit_build_core.setuptools.build_meta\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:158
msgid ""
"The ``scikit_build_core.setuptools.build_meta`` backend adds ``cmake`` (and "
"``ninja``) to the build requirements only when a suitable version is not "
"already available, and supports config-settings (see :ref:`usage-"
"setuptools_options`). If you don't want those, you can use the standard "
"setuptools backend here; then list ``cmake`` (and ``ninja``) in ``requires`` "
"yourself."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:165
msgid ""
"Make a hello folder inside my_project folder and place `_hello.cxx <https://"
"github.com/scikit-build/scikit-build-sample-projects/"
"blob/8fdbc8a0dd78656ea0b431e005b49f3e19786444/projects/hello-cpp/hello/"
"_hello.cxx>`_ and `__init__.py <https://github.com/scikit-build/scikit-build-"
"sample-projects/blob/8fdbc8a0dd78656ea0b431e005b49f3e19786444/projects/hello-"
"cpp/hello/__init__.py>`_ inside hello folder."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:167
msgid ""
"Now everything is ready; go to my_project's parent folder and type the "
"following command to install your extension::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:169
msgid "pip install my_project/."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:171
msgid "If you want to see the detail of installation::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:173
msgid "pip install my_project/. -v"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:175
msgid "Try your new extension::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:177
msgid ""
"$ python\n"
"Python 3.10.4 (main, Jun 29 2022, 12:14:53) [GCC 11.2.0] on linux\n"
"Type \"help\", \"copyright\", \"credits\" or \"license\" for more "
"information.\n"
">>> import hello\n"
">>> hello.hello(\"scikit-build\")\n"
"Hello, scikit-build!\n"
">>>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:187
msgid ""
"By default, scikit-build looks in the project top-level directory for a file "
"named ``CMakeLists.txt``. It will then invoke the ``cmake`` executable, "
"using CMake's default :doc:`generator </generators>` unless the "
"``CMAKE_GENERATOR`` environment variable is set."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:195
msgid "Setup options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:198
msgid "setuptools options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:202
msgid "scikit-build no longer intercepts or rewrites setuptools options."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:204
msgid ""
"Standard setuptools options are handled by setuptools itself and can be "
"declared in ``setup.py``, ``setup.cfg``, or the ``[project]`` table of "
"``pyproject.toml`` as usual. See the `setuptools documentation <https://"
"setuptools.pypa.io/en/latest/userguide/>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:210
msgid "scikit-build options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:212
msgid ""
"Scikit-build augments the ``setup()`` function with the following options:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:214
msgid ""
"``cmake_args``: List of `CMake options <https://cmake.org/cmake/help/latest/"
"manual/cmake.1.html#options>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:216 ../../../usage.rst:237 ../../../usage.rst:251
msgid "For example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:218
msgid ""
"setup(\n"
"  [...]\n"
"  cmake_args=['-DSOME_FEATURE:BOOL=OFF']\n"
"  [...]\n"
"  )"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:224
msgid ""
"``cmake_install_dir``: relative directory where the CMake artifacts are "
"installed. By default, it is set to an empty string."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:228
msgid ""
"``cmake_source_dir``: Relative directory containing the project ``CMakeLists."
"txt``. By default, it is set to the top-level directory where ``setup.py`` "
"is found."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:231
msgid ""
"``cmake_install_target``: Name of the target to \"build\" for installing the "
"artifacts into the wheel. By default, this option is set to ``install``, "
"which is always provided by CMake and runs ``cmake --install``. Any other "
"value is installed by building that target with ``cmake --build --target``, "
"which can be used to only install certain components."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:239
msgid ""
"install(TARGETS foo COMPONENT runtime)\n"
"add_custom_target(foo-install-runtime\n"
"    ${CMAKE_COMMAND}\n"
"    -DCMAKE_INSTALL_COMPONENT=runtime\n"
"    -P \"${PROJECT_BINARY_DIR}/cmake_install.cmake\"\n"
"    DEPENDS foo\n"
"    )"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:247
msgid ""
"``cmake_process_manifest_hook``: Python function consuming the list of files "
"to be installed produced by cmake. For example, "
"``cmake_process_manifest_hook`` can be used to exclude static libraries from "
"the built wheel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:253
msgid ""
"def exclude_static_libraries(cmake_manifest):\n"
"    return list(filter(lambda name: not (name.endswith('.a')), "
"cmake_manifest))\n"
"\n"
"setup(\n"
"  [...]\n"
"  cmake_process_manifest_hook=exclude_static_libraries\n"
"  [...]\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:262
msgid ""
"These options are described in more detail in the `scikit-build-core "
"setuptools plugin documentation <https://scikit-build-core.readthedocs.io/en/"
"latest/plugins/setuptools.html>`__. All other scikit-build-core settings can "
"be set in the ``[tool.scikit-build]`` table of ``pyproject.toml``; see the "
"`scikit-build-core configuration documentation <https://scikit-build-core."
"readthedocs.io/en/latest/configuration/index.html>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:272
msgid ""
"The ``cmake_with_sdist`` option now raises an error if set to ``True``, and "
"the ``cmake_languages`` and ``cmake_minimum_required_version`` options are "
"ignored with a warning. See :ref:`migration_guide`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:280
msgid "Command line options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:284
msgid ""
"The scikit-build-specific command line options and the ``setup.py "
"<setuptools args> -- <cmake args> -- <build tool args>`` syntax were "
"removed. See :ref:`migration_guide`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:288
msgid ""
"Build with a standard frontend like ``pip``, ``build`` or ``uv``. When using "
"the ``scikit_build_core.setuptools.build_meta`` backend (see :ref:"
"`basic_usage_example`), any scikit-build-core setting can be passed as a "
"config-setting::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:293
msgid ""
"pip install . -C cmake.build-type=Debug\n"
"python -m build -C cmake.args=\"-DSOME_FEATURE:BOOL=OFF\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:296
msgid ""
"CMake options can also be passed using the ``CMAKE_ARGS`` environment "
"variable, the ``cmake_args`` keyword of ``setup()``, or scikit-build-core's "
"``[tool.scikit-build]`` settings in ``pyproject.toml``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:303
msgid "Advanced Usage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:306
msgid "How to test if scikit-build is driving the compilation ?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:308
msgid ""
"To support the case of code base being built as both a standalone project "
"and a python wheel, it is possible to test for the variable ``SKBUILD``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:311
msgid ""
"if(SKBUILD)\n"
"  message(STATUS \"The project is built using scikit-build\")\n"
"endif()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:319
msgid ""
"The ``SKBUILD`` variable is now set to ``2`` instead of ``TRUE``. Both "
"values are truthy in CMake."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:325
msgid "Parallel builds"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:327
msgid ""
"The build runs through ``cmake --build``; with the ``Ninja`` generator it is "
"parallel by default. Set the `CMAKE_BUILD_PARALLEL_LEVEL <https://cmake.org/"
"cmake/help/latest/envvar/CMAKE_BUILD_PARALLEL_LEVEL.html>`_ environment "
"variable to control the number of parallel jobs::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:332
msgid "CMAKE_BUILD_PARALLEL_LEVEL=3 pip install ."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:338
msgid "Support for isolated build"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:340
msgid ""
"Build frontends like ``pip`` and ``build`` install the ``build-system."
"requires`` entries from ``pyproject.toml`` into an isolated environment "
"before building. scikit-build supports isolated builds, as well as builds "
"with isolation disabled (``pip install --no-build-isolation``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:347
msgid "Editable installs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:351
msgid "Editable installs previously worked without extra configuration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:353
msgid ""
"In-place builds (``python setup.py build_ext --inplace``) work without extra "
"configuration. Editable installs (``pip install -e .``) require opting in to "
"scikit-build-core's \"inplace\" editable mode in ``pyproject.toml``::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:357
msgid ""
"[tool.scikit-build]\n"
"editable.mode = \"inplace\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:360
msgid ""
"For details, including setuptools' strict editable mode, see the `scikit-"
"build-core setuptools plugin documentation <https://scikit-build-core."
"readthedocs.io/en/latest/plugins/setuptools.html#editable-installs>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:368
msgid "Optimized incremental build"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:370
msgid ""
"To optimize the developer workflow, the CMake build directory is kept inside "
"the standard setuptools ``build/`` directory (an ``_skbuild`` directory "
"under ``build/temp.*``) and reused across builds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:374
msgid ""
"If a file is added to the CMake build system by updating one of the "
"``CMakeLists.txt`` files, the generated build-system will automatically "
"detect the change and reconfigure the project the next time a build is run."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:380
msgid "Environment variable configuration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:382
msgid ""
"Scikit-build support environment variables to configure some options. These "
"are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:384
msgid "``CMAKE_ARGS``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:385
msgid "This will add configuration options when configuring CMake."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:387
msgid "``CMAKE_GENERATOR``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:388
msgid "This selects the CMake generator to use. See :doc:`/generators`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:390
msgid "``SKBUILD_CONFIGURE_OPTIONS``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:391
msgid ""
"Extra arguments appended when configuring CMake, like ``CMAKE_ARGS``. "
"(Deprecated)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:393
msgid "``SKBUILD_BUILD_OPTIONS``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:394
msgid "Extra arguments forwarded to ``cmake --build``. (Deprecated)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:396
msgid ""
"Both ``SKBUILD_*_OPTIONS`` variables are split following shell quoting rules "
"and only honored when building through ``skbuild.setup()``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:399
msgid ""
"In addition, every scikit-build-core setting can be set using a "
"corresponding ``SKBUILD_*`` environment variable. See the `scikit-build-core "
"configuration documentation <https://scikit-build-core.readthedocs.io/en/"
"latest/configuration/index.html>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:407
msgid "Cross-compilation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../usage.rst:409
msgid ""
"Cross-compilation works through the standard CMake mechanisms (`CMake "
"toolchains <https://cmake.org/cmake/help/latest/manual/cmake-toolchains.7."
"html>`_). See the scikit-build-core `cross-compilation guide <https://scikit-"
"build-core.readthedocs.io/en/latest/guide/crosscompile.html>`_; tools like "
"`cibuildwheel <https://cibuildwheel.pypa.io>`_ handle the common macOS and "
"Windows cases automatically."
msgstr ""
